  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 112 PM EST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nNear term forecast generally on track as a cold front continues to \nsweep across the area for the remainder of the afternoon into the \nearly evening hours. A band of showers will persist (albeit in a \nweak state) over the next several hours, lasting for maybe 1 to 2 \nhours at any given location. Upstream rainfall amounts through 18Z \nhave been even lower than forecasted, with most locations\nremaining AOB 0.05\". \n\nThe first shot of cold air arrives in the wake of the front with 850 \nmb temps ranging from 0 to -4 C by 12Z Mon. Although a favorable NW \nflow will be in place and Delta Ts should be in the mid teens, low \nlevel moisture will be on the wane (925 mb RH at or below 70%), \nresulting in mainly some lake effect clouds, but no precip through \nMonday. A secondary trough arrives Monday night with additional cold \nair (-5 to -8 C by 12 Tue) sweeping in. Moisture remains very \nlimited, but the combination of the strong trough and increasing \nomega may squeeze out a very narrow window of snow showers near the \nLake MI shoreline Monday evening before moving into NE IL. High \ntemperatures in the 40s and lows at or below freezing will remain \nentrenched in the area into next weekend with the coldest temps \nTuesday and Tuesday night. \n\nModels remain fairly consistent on a stronger northern stream low \nevolving in a larger longwave trough as it enters the Dakotas and \ndives SE to Iowa by 00Z Thu. A stronger vort max will translate in \nthe southern stream with models varying on timing of phasing of this \nenergy with the northern low. By 6Z Fri, EC/GFS agree on a 540dm-ish \nupper low located somewhere between western OH (EC) to SW \nPennsylvania (GFS). Strong lift and moisture advection will take \nplace as all of this is coming together with fairly high confidence \non a band of rain moving towards the general area sometime in the \nWednesday night to Thanksgiving day period with an extended period \nof wrap around precip possible into Friday before the upper low. \nDiurnally driven ptype issues will likely exist (rain/snow or all \nsnow at night and just rain during the day), but plenty of time to \nsort out finer details in the coming days.",
